NetHub is designed as a control plane and set of protocol definitions for
self-hosted remote access to open-source routers and firewalls. This NOTICE
file is provided in accordance with Section 4(d) of the Apache License,
Version 2.0, and lists the third-party components that NetHub is designed to
distribute with, or vendor/bundle.

================================================================================
Third-party components
================================================================================

Deployment helper images rebuild Caddy 2.11.7 (Apache-2.0) with its standard
modules and golang.org/x/net 0.60.0, and gosu 1.19 (Apache-2.0) from upstream
commit 6456aaa0f3c854d199d0f037f068eb97515b7513. Both use Go 1.26.9.
Caddy's upstream copyright is Copyright 2015 Matthew Holt and The Caddy Authors.
The linked module versions are recorded in deploy/images/*/go.mod and go.sum.
Their license and notice files, together with the Go license, are collected
into the final helper images. Base images retain their upstream components
and licensing. See deploy/images/README.md for the build changes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
frp (fast reverse proxy)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Homepage:  https://github.com/fatedier/frp
  Version:   0.71.1 (historical Weekend 0 measurements used 0.71.0)
  License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
  Copyright: Copyright 2016 fatedier, <fatedier@gmail.com>

  NetHub is designed to vendor the `frpc` client binary as the sole
  persistent process on the device side (to be run from within the OPNsense
  agent plugin), and to run `frps` as the relay/server component of the
  NetHub control plane. frp is intended to be used unmodified, with only
  NetHub-specific configuration; no changes to frp's own source are planned.
  Its full license text is available at
  https://github.com/fatedier/frp/blob/master/LICENSE and is reproduced in
  full in the Apache License, Version 2.0 referenced above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Go modules are pinned in server/go.mod and server/go.sum. Console npm packages
are pinned in server/web/package.json and package-lock.json. Each dependency
retains its own license. The console build includes runtime dependency license
texts at /third-party-licenses.txt; the build fails if a runtime package lacks
an included license file.

